VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-026
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of abdominal distension, severe lethargy, and mild confusion requiring urgent after-hours evaluation to exclude spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and manage impending decompensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage confirmed rapidly worsening abdominal girth over 48 hours, accompanied by new-onset somnolence and altered sleep-wake cycles. This necessitated an urgent medical assessment to evaluate for acute hepatic encephalopathy and severe third-spacing fluid shifts, which could not be safely delayed until the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Massive stomach swelling, yellow skin, and overwhelming exhaustion. Patient states, My stomach is so tight I can barely breathe, and I feel completely drained of energy.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have evolved markedly over the past week in the context of chronic Hepatitis C infection. Initially, the patient noted increasing fatigue and reduced appetite. This progressed three days ago to tense ascites producing marked abdominal distension, causing significant discomfort and dyspnea when supine. Concurrently, the patient developed significant, symmetrical bilateral lower limb pitting edema. Today, the family noted new-onset jaundice presenting as icteric sclerae and yellowing of the skin, alongside mild hepatic encephalopathy manifesting as altered sleep-wake cycles and intermittent confusion. The patient reports easy bruising but denies overt gastrointestinal bleeding.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe abdominal distension and profound fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk secondary to encephalopathy and altered center of gravity; adult child is providing continuous supervision.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 85.0 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically ill, cachectic with sarcopenia evident in the temporalis muscles, and visibly jaundiced.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. JVP is not elevated. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally, though diaphragmatic excursion is limited by ascites. Gastrointestinal: The diagnostic triad is evident. Examination reveals tense ascites with shifting dullness and a palpable fluid thrill. Caput medusae, characterized by distended, engorged superficial periumbilical veins, is prominent. Scleral icterus/jaundice is overtly present. Splenomegaly is palpable below the left costal margin. Neurological: Mild asterixis is demonstrable upon wrist extension.
Environmental Observations: Safe indoor environment, clear pathway to the bathroom, commode provided beside the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has missed recent doses of Spironolactone and Lactulose due to lethargy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Liver Cirrhosis (Chronic Hepatitis C) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Right Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of elevated jugular venous pressure and the presence of stigmata specific to chronic liver disease (caput medusae, spider angiomata).

Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, rigors, or acute diffuse abdominal tenderness, though a diagnostic paracentesis remains indicated if clinical deterioration occurs.

Malignant Biliary Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The known history of Hepatitis C cirrhosis, massive third-spacing, and encephalopathy dominating the clinical picture rather than painless, isolated jaundice.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits signs of acute-on-chronic hepatic decompensation with massive third-spacing of fluid into the peritoneal cavity and early grade 1 hepatic encephalopathy. The failure of the liver to synthesize albumin and manage portal pressures has resulted in tense ascites and critical localized fluid shifts. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have risked progression to severe unarousable encephalopathy or respiratory compromise secondary to diaphragmatic splinting. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively assess neurological status, rule out acute intra-abdominal infection, and immediately re-initiate and titrate diuretic and laxative therapies to safely bridge the patient to formal hepatology review. The expected investigation results include positive HCV RNA viral load with an ultrasound demonstrating a nodular, shrunken liver and reversal of portal vein flow that would assist in confirming the diagnosis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Lactulose 30mL PO immediately to target encephalopathy. Instructed patient to resume Spironolactone 100mg PO daily. Advised strict dietary sodium restriction.
Positive Behavioral Change: To gain control over your fluid buildup and confusion, commit to taking the prescribed lactulose syrup specifically to achieve three soft bowel movements daily. Keep a tally chart in the bathroom. When you achieve the third movement and notice your thinking becoming clearer, explicitly recognize that you are actively flushing toxins from your system. This direct cognitive improvement will serve as the reward for adhering strictly to the medication regimen.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to Community Nursing for daily weight monitoring and clinical review.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours for serum electrolytes, renal function, and liver function tests.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately or call 000 if you begin vomiting blood or passing black, tarry stools, if your confusion worsens significantly, or if you develop a fever and severe abdominal pain.

